#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# number_form

#% text
# `number_form(x)` shows the machine reals in `x` to six significant digits; `number_form(x, n)` to `n`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Numbers from 10^-5 to 10^6 are written out; larger and smaller ones as `m × 10^e`.

#% item
# The argument is evaluated first; `number_form` stays around the value as a wrapper that changes only how it is shown.

#% item
# Only machine reals change: integers, fractions and symbols are shown as they are.

#% item
# The pages keep values as text that reads back, so they show the wrapper; `mc-repl` and the notebook show `330809.`, `5.18 × 10^11`, `3.14159265359` and `[0.000123, 2/3, 1 × 10^-9]` for the examples below.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
number_form(330808.99)
#% output
#= 330809.

#% cell
number_form(5.18e11)
#% output
#= 5.18 × 10^11

#% cell
number_form(pi + 0.0, 12)
#% output
#= 3.14159265359

#% subsection
# Scope

#% cell
number_form([0.000123456789, 2/3, 1.0e-9], 3)
#% output
#= [0.000123, 2/3, 1 × 10^-9]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `scientific_form`, `input_form`, `n`
